WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.814
Alert triage is the process of reviewing new events, determining priority, and deciding what action is needed.

00:00:07.814 --> 00:00:17.318
Priority should reflect potential impact, affected asset, evidence quality, urgency, and current context rather than simply repeating a vendor severity label.

00:00:17.318 --> 00:00:25.978
Confidence describes how strongly a tool supports its observation; it does not prove the event is important or correct.

00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:31.680
Show confidence with the rule, model, threshold, source quality, and relevant evidence.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.383
A high-confidence detection on an unimportant condition may still be low priority.

00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:44.775
Group related events so a reviewer sees one coherent incident instead of dozens of isolated alerts.

00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:50.055
Shared assets, accounts, locations, time windows, or indicators can support grouping.

00:00:50.055 --> 00:00:54.067
Preserve individual evidence and make automated relationships explainable.

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:59.770
False positives are alerts that incorrectly identify a problem or meaningful event.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:09.696
Reviewers need a clear way to mark them, record why they were wrong, and route that feedback to rule or model owners.

00:01:09.696 --> 00:01:16.666
Suppression should be narrow and time-bound so tuning does not create an unnoticed blind spot.

00:01:16.666 --> 00:01:23.213
Escalation paths should identify who handles safety, physical security, cyber, privacy, or operational issues.

00:01:23.213 --> 00:01:26.804
Important actions should require appropriate human confirmation.

00:01:26.804 --> 00:01:34.618
The dashboard should not automatically make high-impact decisions about people solely from uncertain visual or behavioral inferences.

00:01:34.618 --> 00:01:39.053
Document acknowledgments, assignments, evidence reviewed, decisions, and closure reasons.

00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:42.644
This creates accountability and supports later learning.

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:52.570
A mature feedback loop uses review outcomes to improve thresholds, source quality, grouping, and playbooks while monitoring whether changes increase missed events.
